  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What is the downside of a 529 plan?",
      "answer": "The main downsides are the missing federal deduction and the cost of non-qualified withdrawals. Contributions are not deductible on your federal return, and earnings withdrawn for non-qualified spending are taxed as income plus a 10% additional tax. Contributions also cannot exceed what the beneficiary's qualified expenses need, and caps apply to room and board and to K-12 tuition."
    },
    {
      "question": "What happens to a 529 if the child doesn't go to college?",
      "answer": "The money stays under the account owner's control and has several tax-free routes. The owner can change the beneficiary to a member of the beneficiary's family with no income tax, or roll the money to another family member's 529. Up to $35,000 over a lifetime can move to the beneficiary's Roth IRA if the account has been open at least 15 years and the other conditions are met. Up to $10,000 lifetime can pay qualified student loans. Registered apprenticeship costs also count as qualified expenses. A non-qualified withdrawal returns contributions untaxed, while the earnings are taxed as income plus the 10% additional tax unless an exception applies."
    },
    {
      "question": "Who owns the money in a 529 account?",
      "answer": "The person who buys the plan controls the money. The IRS says whoever purchases the plan is the custodian and controls the funds until they are withdrawn. The beneficiary is the person the money is meant for, and the owner can change the beneficiary to a family member without income tax."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can a child have multiple 529 plans?",
      "answer": "Yes. The IRS says there is no limit on the number of 529 plans you can set up, and each account names one designated beneficiary. The $20,000 annual K-12 limit counts withdrawals from all of the beneficiary's plans together."
    },
    {
      "question": "Are 529 contributions tax deductible?",
      "answer": "Not on your federal return. IRS Publication 970 says you cannot deduct payments or contributions to a qualified tuition program, and the IRS 529 Q&A repeats that contributions are not deductible. State treatment of contributions varies by state."
    },
    {
      "question": "What can 529 funds be used for?",
      "answer": "529 funds can pay qualified education expenses tax free. That list covers tuition and fees, books, supplies and equipment, room and board for students enrolled at least half-time (up to a cap), and computer equipment, software and internet access used by the beneficiary while enrolled. It also covers registered apprenticeship costs, certain credentialing expenses, up to $10,000 lifetime of qualified student loans, and up to $20,000 per year of K-12 tuition."
    }
  ],
